# Copy to .env and edit. No real secrets belong in this file.

# --- Server ---
PORT=8080
# Public origin of the store, used to build absolute URLs. No trailing slash.
BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
LOG_LEVEL=info
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15

# --- Database ---
# For running the binary on the host (`make run`, `make seed`). The compose
# server ignores this and talks to its own postgres service, so copying this
# file cannot accidentally point the container at the wrong database.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://gostore:gostore@localhost:5432/gostore?sslmode=disable
# Used only by the test suite. Tests are skipped when it is unset.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://gostore:gostore@localhost:5432/gostore?sslmode=disable

# --- Store ---
STORE_NAME=gostore
CURRENCY=ZAR

# --- Admin ---
# Both required. Generate a matching pair with:
#   read -rs P && printf %s "$P" | go run ./cmd/hashpw
# The password itself is never stored or configured — only its bcrypt hash.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# 32+ random bytes, base64. `openssl rand -base64 32` also works.
# Changing it signs the admin out everywhere, which is the only revocation
# mechanism a stateless session has.
SESSION_SECRET=
# Optional. How long a sign-in lasts; defaults to 24.
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS=24
# Optional, and only during a rotation: the outgoing SESSION_SECRET. Sessions
# signed with it still verify, but new ones are signed with SESSION_SECRET, so
# rotating does not sign the admin out. Remove it after SESSION_TTL_HOURS.
# SESSION_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# BASE_URL matters beyond absolute links: it decides whether cookies are marked
# Secure, and the scheme the CSRF origin check compares against. Getting it wrong
# on an HTTPS deployment refuses every form with a 403.

# --- Payments (PayFast) ---
# Both required. The values below are PayFast's own published sandbox
# credentials, which appear in their documentation — they are not secret, and
# they take no real money. Replace all three with your own before going live.
#   Sandbox dashboard: https://sandbox.payfast.co.za
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_ID=10000100
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_KEY=46f0cd694581a
# The account's "salt passphrase". It must match the PayFast dashboard exactly:
# set on one side only and every signature fails. jt7NOE43FZPn is the sandbox's.
PAYFAST_PASSPHRASE=jt7NOE43FZPn
# Defaults to true. Set it to false only with live credentials in place — it is
# the difference between a test transaction and somebody's actual card.
PAYFAST_SANDBOX=true
#
# Return, cancel and notify URLs are derived from BASE_URL, so there is nothing
# to keep in step. The exception is the notify URL, which PayFast's *servers*
# must reach: localhost is not reachable from the internet, so testing a real
# payment locally needs a tunnel, and this is where its hostname goes.
#   e.g. PAYFAST_NOTIFY_URL=https://abc123.ngrok.app/payments/payfast/callback
# PAYFAST_NOTIFY_URL=
#
# Optional. Comma-separated CIDRs a notification may come from, overriding
# PayFast's published ranges — they have changed before, and adding one should
# not need a new release. The literal value "any" disables the check: it is for
# testing against the sandbox, which does not necessarily notify from the
# production ranges, and it is never right in production.
# PAYFAST_ALLOWED_CIDRS=any

# --- Email ---
# Optional, but a store that sends no receipts is a poor one. SMTP_HOST and
# EMAIL_FROM must be set together, or the server refuses to start: half-configured
# mail is the case worth catching at boot rather than at the first paid order.
# With neither set, confirmations are logged and dropped, and the server says so
# loudly at startup.
#
# The compose stack points at mailpit, which catches every message and shows it at
# http://localhost:8025 — nothing is delivered to a real address in development.
SMTP_HOST=localhost
SMTP_PORT=1025
# starttls (default, correct for port 587) | tls (implicit, port 465) | none.
# "none" sends credentials and order details in the clear: mailpit only.
SMTP_TLS=none
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# The sender. A relay will usually reject a From on a domain it is not responsible
# for, so this has to be an address it accepts.
EMAIL_FROM=orders@example.com
# Optional, when replies should go somewhere other than EMAIL_FROM.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
#
# Optional. Where a copy of each paid order goes — whoever packs the parcel. It
# also carries the oversell warning, which otherwise only exists in the logs.
# Leave it empty and the customer's receipt is the only mail sent.
ORDER_NOTIFY_EMAIL=shop@example.com

# --- Product images (object storage) ---
# Optional. Without these the admin takes a pasted image URL, which is a complete
# answer for a shop whose photographs are already hosted somewhere. Set them and
# the admin uploads instead. They are all-or-nothing: BLOB_ENDPOINT with any of the
# rest missing is a boot failure, because the alternative is finding out at the
# first upload.
#
# Works against anything speaking the S3 API. For Cloudflare R2:
#   BLOB_ENDPOINT=<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   BLOB_REGION=auto
# For Google Cloud Storage in interoperability mode:
#   BLOB_ENDPOINT=storage.googleapis.com
# The compose stack points at its own MinIO.
BLOB_ENDPOINT=localhost:9000
BLOB_BUCKET=gostore-images
BLOB_ACCESS_KEY_ID=gostore
BLOB_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=gostore123
# "auto" is what R2 wants; GCS and MinIO ignore it.
BLOB_REGION=auto
# false only for a MinIO on the same machine. Credentials otherwise go in the clear.
BLOB_USE_TLS=false
#
# Where images are *read* from, which is not where they are written to. The server
# writes through BLOB_ENDPOINT; a browser fetches through this. For R2 it is your
# custom domain or the pub-*.r2.dev address; in compose it is MinIO's own.
#
# The bucket has to be publicly readable at this address — images are served
# straight from it and never proxied through the store, which is what lets a CDN
# do the work.
BLOB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:9000/gostore-images

# --- Proxies ---
# Set this only if something in front of the server actually sets
# X-Forwarded-For, and make sure it *replaces* the header rather than appending
# to it. Otherwise a client can claim any source IP it likes — which is one of
# the checks on the payment callback.
# TRUST_PROXY_IP=false

# --- Embedding ---
# Optional. Comma-separated origins allowed to fetch the read-only catalog
# fragments cross-origin, for dropping the catalog into a page hosted elsewhere.
# Empty means no CORS headers and no framing at all. Use scheme://host[:port]
# with no trailing slash; "*" allows any origin, which is safe only because the
# fragments are cookie-free and read-only.
# EMBED_ORIGINS=https://www.example.com

# --- Theming ---
# Optional. Templates in this directory override the embedded defaults by name.
# TEMPLATE_DIR=./templates
